const _body = "<p>A contract is a JSON file, <code>contract.json</code>. It sits next to your Rust implementation and describes what the capability accepts, what it produces, and what conditions are expected to hold on both sides. You register the contract and the compiled WASM binary together, as part of a registry bundle.</p>\n\n      <h2>Minimal contract structure</h2>\n      <pre><code>{\n  \"kind\": \"capability_contract\",\n  \"schema_version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"id\": \"examples.pricing.calculate-price\",\n  \"namespace\": \"examples.pricing\",\n  \"name\": \"calculate-price\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"lifecycle\": \"draft\",\n  \"service_type\": \"stateless\",\n  \"artifact_type\": \"native\",\n  \"description\": \"Calculates the total price for a line item\",\n  \"input_schema\": {\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"required\": [\"quantity\", \"unit_price\"],\n    \"properties\": {\n      \"quantity\": { \"type\": \"integer\", \"minimum\": 1 },\n      \"unit_price\": { \"type\": \"number\", \"minimum\": 0.0 }\n    }\n  },\n  \"output_schema\": {\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"required\": [\"total\"],\n    \"properties\": {\n      \"total\": { \"type\": \"number\" }\n    }\n  },\n  \"preconditions\": [\n    { \"id\": \"quantity-positive\", \"description\": \"quantity must be greater than 0\" }\n  ],\n  \"postconditions\": [\n    { \"id\": \"total-non-negative\", \"description\": \"total must be greater than or equal to 0\" }\n  ],\n  \"execution\": {\n    \"binary_format\": \"wasm\",\n    \"entrypoint\": { \"kind\": \"wasi-command\", \"command\": \"run\" },\n    \"preferred_targets\": [\"local\"],\n    \"constraints\": {\n      \"host_api_access\": \"none\",\n      \"network_access\": \"forbidden\",\n      \"filesystem_access\": \"none\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"provenance\": {\n    \"spec_refs\": [\"002-capability-contracts\"],\n    \"exception_refs\": []\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n\n      <h2>What each field does</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><code>id</code>, <code>namespace</code>, <code>name</code> — the identity triple used by the registry and MCP server; <code>id</code> must equal <code>namespace</code> + \".\" + <code>name</code></li>\n        <li><code>input_schema</code> — JSON Schema for the input object the caller must provide</li>\n        <li><code>output_schema</code> — JSON Schema for the output the WASM binary must return</li>\n        <li><code>preconditions</code> — assertions expected to hold before execution; each is an id and a description. These are documentation for now, not something the runtime evaluates before invocation</li>\n        <li><code>postconditions</code> — same structure, for the state after execution; also documentation-only rather than runtime-enforced today</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Registering the contract</h2>\n      <p>Add the contract and its compiled WASM module to a registry bundle manifest, then inspect and register the bundle:</p>\n      <pre><code>cargo run -p traverse-cli-rs -- bundle inspect path/to/manifest.json\ncargo run -p traverse-cli-rs -- bundle register path/to/manifest.json</code></pre>\n\n      <p><code>bundle inspect</code> validates the contract's JSON structure before anything is registered — a missing required field, or an <code>id</code> that doesn't match <code>namespace.name</code>, is rejected there rather than at registration time.</p>\n\n      <p>See <a href=\"/questions/what-are-preconditions.html\">what are preconditions</a> and <a href=\"/questions/what-are-postconditions.html\">what are postconditions</a> for more detail on writing conditions.</p>";
const _jsonLd = "{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"FAQPage\",\"mainEntity\":[{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"How do I write a capability contract?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"A capability contract is a contract.json file that defines the capability's identity, input schema, output schema, preconditions, postconditions, and execution constraints. You write it alongside your Rust implementation and register both together, as part of a registry bundle, with the Traverse CLI.\"}}]}";
